        "heading_style": "caps",
        "heading": "THE TRUTH ABOUT ESCAPE ARTIST DOGS",
        "text": "<p><strong>our dog doesn't mean to run.</strong> The door opens for two seconds, a squirrel darts by, the gate's left cracked — and he's gone. Down the driveway, into the street, three houses down before you've got your shoes on.</p><p>And every time, your stomach drops. You're chasing him down the block, praying a car doesn't come around the corner. <strong>It only takes once.</strong></p><p>So you try to fix it. A $30 shock collar off Amazon he figures out in a week. A trainer who wants $2,000 and eight sessions. A fence quote that makes you laugh out loud — <strong>$6,000 to bury wire in your yard</strong>, and he'd still dig under it.</p><p>That's the trap: expensive fixes that don't work, or cheap ones that don't last. Either way <strong>you're still standing at the window every time he's outside</strong>, unable to relax.</p><p>The truth? Your dog doesn't need a wall. <strong>He needs a boundary he understands — and a little guidance when he forgets it.</strong><br/></p><p>What if you could set that boundary in an afternoon, <strong>train him from your hand anywhere you go, and finally stop chasing him down the street?</strong></p>",
        "mobile_center": true,
        "media_shadow": false,
